course: dictionary
lesson: 1204
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: One word can have more than one meaning, and the words around it tell you which one is meant. The word bud is a good example. «Horse chestnut buds are covered in sticky stuff that keeps out insects and cold.» Here a bud is a small swelling on a plant that will open into a leaf or a flower.
explain: Now read this: «Taste buds for sweet, salty, sour, bitter and savoury are found all over the tongue.» Taste buds are not plant parts at all. They are tiny parts of your tongue that sense flavours. Same word, different meaning, and the context tells you which.
explain: When a word has several meanings, try each one in the sentence and see which makes sense. «Teenager, bud.» Here bud is an informal, friendly word for a friend or a boy, mostly used in America. It has nothing to do with plants.
explain: Watch out for verbs too. «The trees are budding» means the trees are growing buds, ready to open. A budding artist is someone just starting out who may become good. Check the clues before you decide what the word means.
fact: A bud is a small swelling on a plant that will open into a leaf or a flower.
fact: To bud means to grow buds, as in «the trees are budding».
fact: Taste buds are tiny parts of your tongue that sense flavours: sweet, sour, salty, bitter and savoury.
fact: «Nip it in the bud» means stop a problem early, before it grows.
fact: «Bud» (informal, mostly American) is a friendly word for a friend or a boy.
exercise: «Horse chestnut buds are covered in sticky stuff that keeps out insects and cold.» What is a bud on a plant? | A friendly word for a boy | A small insect | *A small swelling that will open into a leaf or a flower | A type of tongue
exercise: «Teenager, bud.» What does bud mean in that sentence? | A flower that is closed | A growing tree | *A friendly word for a friend or a boy | A part of the tongue
exercise: «Taste buds for sweet, salty, sour, bitter and savoury are found all over the tongue.» What are taste buds? | Small swellings on a plant | Friendly words for a boy | Tiny seeds that grow into trees | *Tiny parts of your tongue that sense flavours
test: «The trees are budding.» What does budding mean here? | Falling off the tree | *Growing buds, ready to open into leaves | Growing very tall quickly | Singing loudly
test: What does «nip it in the bud» mean? | *Stop a problem early | Bite something small | Start a big project | Pick a flower
exam: «Potatoes are swollen underground stems that store food, and the eyes on a potato are buds that can grow into new plants.» In this sentence, what is a bud? | A friendly word for a friend | *A small swelling that can grow into a new plant | A taste on your tongue | A flower petal
